Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{2} + \frac{y}{6} &= \frac{5}{12} \\
\frac{x}{4} - \frac{y}{6} &= 0
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
-\frac{x}{3} + \frac{y}{2} &= \frac{1}{4} \\
\frac{x}{3} - \frac{y}{4} &= 0
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{3} + \frac{y}{5} &= \frac{3}{10} \\
\frac{x}{6} - \frac{y}{5} &= 0
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{5} - \frac{y}{3} &= \frac{7}{15} \\
\frac{x}{2} + \frac{y}{3} &= 0
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{3} + \frac{y}{4} &= \frac{5}{12} \\
\frac{x}{6} - \frac{y}{4} &= 0
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{3} - \frac{3y}{4} &= \frac{5}{2} \\
\frac{2x}{5} + \frac{y}{2} &= \frac{1}{5}
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{2} - \frac{y}{3} &= \frac{5}{6} \\
\frac{2x}{3} + \frac{3y}{4} &= -\frac{1}{12}
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{3x}{4} - \frac{2y}{5} &= \frac{3}{10} \\
\frac{x}{3} + \frac{y}{2} &= \frac{13}{6}
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{2} - \frac{y}{3} &= \frac{1}{6} \\
\frac{x}{4} + \frac{y}{6} &= \frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}

Solve by the addition method:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{x}{3} &= \frac{13}{12} - \frac{y}{4} \\
\frac{x}{2} - \frac{y}{6} &= \frac{13}{6}
\end{aligned}

Solve the system of equations by the substitution method:
\frac{2}{3}x + \frac{1}{2}y = 1
\frac{3}{4}x - \frac{1}{3}y = -\frac{17}{4}

qs.s:
A linear system has one solution when the lines have different slopes, requiring \( rac{4}{8} 
eq  rac{-3}{k}\). Simplifying to \( rac{1}{2} 
eq  rac{-3}{k}\) yields \(k 
eq -6\).
